1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:05,934
Watching Wildlife Responsibly At the reserve an instructor explained the difference between

2
00:00:05,947 --> 00:00:10,145
a mammal and a reptile before leading us past the observation enclosure

3
00:00:11,053 --> 00:00:14,947
A peacock displayed its feathers a turtle rested near the pond and

4
00:00:14,961 --> 00:00:19,184
a beetle crawled beneath a stone In the distance a herd moved

5
00:00:19,197 --> 00:00:23,842
slowly across the pasture We were told to observe quietly and let

6
00:00:23,855 --> 00:00:29,684
each creature follow its normal pattern The afternoon talk concerned endangered animals

7
00:00:30,592 --> 00:00:35,079
A gorilla a leopard and a whale require very different habitats yet

8
00:00:35,092 --> 00:00:40,237
each can suffer when human activity destroys its surroundings A panther in

9
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:43,237
a photograph was not a pet or a spectacle it was a

10
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:48,211
wild animal whose needs deserved respect The guide also discussed how an

11
00:00:48,224 --> 00:00:53,711
extinct species differs from one merely absent from a particular locality What

12
00:00:53,724 --> 00:00:58,526
impressed us most was the restraint required of a responsible observer We

13
00:00:58,539 --> 00:01:02,158
must not use bait to attract wildlife allow a puppy to chase

14
00:01:02,171 --> 00:01:07,684
birds or toss food into an enclosure Curiosity should lead to understanding

15
00:01:07,697 --> 00:01:12,461
rather than interference The privilege of seeing an animal does not entitle

16
00:01:12,474 --> 00:01:14,105
us to control its behaviour
